.he "ftpd (8)                      11/03/26                      ftpd (8)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
ftpd
- the file transfer server
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd 2
/bin/ftpd [ port ]
.br
/bin/ftpd -i <peer> <pid>
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Ftpd serves one control connection per process.  The caller signs in
with USER and PASS; the machine's accounts are its own business, and
this server accepts the pair and runs the file operations as the user
ftpd was started as.
.sp
Data connections are passive: PASV opens a listener on a port above
20000 and the reply names it.  LIST and NLST send the directory's
names, RETR sends a file, STOR receives one, and each closes the data
connection and answers 226.  PORT is refused - a client may not name a
listening socket here.
.sp
The other commands are QUIT, SYST, PWD, CWD, CDUP, TYPE, MODE, STRU,
NOOP and ABOR.
.sp
The port defaults to 21; the command-line port exists for the
simulator, which binds a high host port for the guest.  Under inetd
the connection is already on fd 0 and the arguments are the caller's
dotted quad and inetd's pid.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ftp (1), inetd (8), socket (3)
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
BUGS
.in +5
.br
The password is not checked, the listing is bare names, and PASS/ACCT
and the ASCII transfer type do not change what the bytes are.
